# --- project data and run artifacts (never committed) ---
data/
runs/

# BenchmarkDotNet writes its logs, csv and html per run. The numbers that
# matter are transcribed into docs/reports/, where they can carry the machine
# and the runtime they were measured on; the raw artifacts are a run record,
# like runs/ above.
dotnet/BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts/

# --- model artifacts (release artifacts are handled separately) ---
*.nrm

# One exception: a hand-built 581-byte fixture model, not trained on anything,
# holding no credential and no data derived from one. It is committed because
# CI has no trained model and no corpus, so without it the binary reader, the
# tree walk and the calibration — the parts most likely to differ between
# Windows and Linux — would be covered on neither.
!tests/fixtures/fixture-model-v1.nrm

# The release model, committed on Frank's decision of 2026-10-22, which lifted
# the "not in version control until final verification" condition of NR-0005 §7.
# It is embedded in the NuGet package, so `dotnet pack` must be reproducible
# from a clean checkout; keeping the weights out while shipping them in the
# package would mean the published artefact could not be rebuilt from source.
# 95 KB of float32 leaves and int16 node indices — no credential strings.
!models/neburedaction-v0.1.0.nrm

# --- credential-shaped test fixtures ---
#
# The generator produces format-correct vendor credentials on purpose, so a
# committed fixture corpus trips GitHub's push protection — which is a control
# this project wants enabled, not excluded. Fixtures are therefore generated on
# demand and never committed; they are deterministic from the seed, so tests
# build them in a temporary directory instead of reading them from git.
#
# tools/check_synthetic_manifest.py still guards this path: anything that does
# appear here must be a registered, generator-produced value, and an end-to-end
# test proves the guard works in both directions.
tests/fixtures/synthetic/
tests/fixtures/synthetic-manifest.json

